The radiation energy density \(u = \frac{4 \sigma T^{4}}{c^{3}}\), where \(\sigma = 5.67 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{W} \cdot \text{m}^{-2} \cdot \text{K}^{-4}\) is the Stefan-Boltzmann constant. According to Einstein's mass-energy equivalence formula, the amount of blackbody radiation per unit volume can be calculated as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}
u & = \frac{4 \sigma T^{4}}{c^{3}} = \frac{4 \times 5.67 \times 10^{-8} \times (10^{10})^{4}}{(3 \times 10^{8})^{3}} \\
& \approx 0.7 \times 10^{8} \, \text{kg} \cdot \text{m}^{-3} = 0.1 \, \text{t} \cdot \text{cm}^{-3}
\end{aligned}
$$

The answer is (A).

