sol_answer:
The total energy of the \(J/\psi\) in the laboratory frame is

\[
E_{J/\psi}=\sqrt{(pc)^2+(Mc^2)^2}
=\sqrt{(100)^2+(3.097)^2}\ \text{GeV}
=100.04795\ \text{GeV}.
\]

For a symmetric two-body decay, the electron and positron have equal laboratory-frame energies. By energy conservation,

\[
E_{e^-}=E_{e^+}=\frac{E_{J/\psi}}{2}
=\frac{100.04795}{2}
=50.02397\ \text{GeV}.
\]

So the final answer is \(\boxed{50.024}\)(GeV).

