reference_answer:
(1) The Hamiltonian for the interaction of each hydrogen nucleus spin with the external magnetic field is given by

$$
H = -\mu \cdot H_{0} = -g \mu_{N} S_{z} H_{0} = -g \mu_{N} H_{0} S_{z}
$$

Therefore, there are two states: $\left|S_{z}=\frac{1}{2}\right\rangle$ and $\left|S_{z}=-\frac{1}{2}\right\rangle$. The energies are, respectively,

$$
E_{1/2} = -\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0}, \quad E_{-1/2} = \frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0}
$$

where $g$ is the Landau factor of the proton, $\mu_{N}$ is the nuclear magnetic moment, and $\mu_{N} = \frac{e \hbar}{2 M_{p} c}$. According to the condition of statistical equilibrium, the probabilities of being in each state at temperature $T$ are

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \left|S_{z}=\frac{1}{2}\right\rangle: \frac{\exp \left(\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)}{\exp \left(\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)+\exp \left(-\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)} \\
& \left|S_{z}=-\frac{1}{2}\right\rangle: \frac{\exp \left(-\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)}{\exp \left(\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)+\exp \left(-\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

These also represent the percentage of the number of protons in each state.

Reference answer:
\boxed{\frac{\exp\left(\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)}{\exp\left(\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)+\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)}, \frac{\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)}{\exp\left(\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)+\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2} g \mu_{N} H_{0} / k T\right)}}

